course: biology
lesson: 241
title: Team players and partners: meerkats, naked mole rats, huddles, cleaners (and do lemmings jump?)
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some animals survive because they work as a team, like a football side where one keeps goal while the others run.
explain: In a meerkat group one meerkat stands on a rock as a guard while the rest dig for beetles, and grown-ups even teach the pups how to eat a scorpion safely.
explain: Naked mole rats live underground with one queen, like bees, and emperor penguin fathers squeeze together in a huge huddle to survive the Antarctic winter.
explain: Other teams are made of two different kinds of animal: a little cleaner fish picks parasites off a big fish, and a bird rides a buffalo eating its ticks; both sides gain something.
explain: And the story that lemmings jump off cliffs together is not true: it was made popular by a 1958 nature film in which the lemmings were pushed.
fact: One meerkat stands guard as a sentinel, watching for eagles and jackals, and gives different alarm calls for danger from the air and from the ground; meerkats that have just eaten well guard more often.
fact: Adult meerkats teach pups to eat scorpions step by step: first dead ones, then ones with the sting removed, then live ones.
fact: Real meerkats live in groups of up to about 50 in southern Africa, led by a dominant female who has most of the pups while the others help to raise them.
fact: Naked mole rats live underground in East Africa in colonies with one queen, like ants and bees; they can live over 30 years and hardly ever get cancer.
fact: Naked mole rats pile up in heaps to keep warm, because they are very poor at keeping their own bodies warm; in experiments they survived about 18 minutes with no oxygen.
fact: Each naked mole rat colony has its own chirp dialect, set by the queen, and a stranger with the wrong «accent» is attacked; a pup moved to another colony as a baby learns that colony's dialect instead.
fact: Prairie dogs have alarm calls that seem to describe a predator, even a person's size and the colour of their clothes, as experiments by Con Slobodchikoff suggested.
fact: Male emperor penguins huddle in thousands, each with an egg on his feet under a warm fold of belly skin, taking turns at the cold edge; inside the huddle it can be more than 20 degrees warmer than outside, and the fathers eat nothing for about two months.
fact: Cleaner wrasse eat parasites off bigger fish at «cleaning stations»; sometimes they cheat by biting healthy skin, and they cheat less when other fish are watching.
fact: Oxpeckers eat ticks and flies off buffaloes and rhinos and hiss when danger comes, but they also peck at wounds to drink blood, so they are partly helpers and partly pests.
fact: Lemmings do not jump off cliffs together; their numbers rise and fall in cycles, and the myth spread through the 1958 Disney film «White Wilderness», where filmmakers pushed lemmings off a bank.
exercise: Why does one meerkat stand on a rock while the others dig? | It is the guard watching for eagles and jackals | It is the leader resting on a high seat while the others do the work | It is warming its belly in the morning sun | It is waiting for its turn to dig
exercise: Which meerkat is most likely to take a turn as guard? | One that has just eaten well | The youngest pup in the group | One that is too ill to dig any more | Always the same old male, every day
exercise: How do grown-up meerkats teach pups to eat scorpions? | Step by step: dead ones, then stingless ones, then live ones | They give pups live scorpions with stings on the very first day they eat | They never let pups eat scorpions at all | They show pups only scorpions that are already eaten
exercise: Who usually has most of the pups in a meerkat group? | The dominant female | Every female equally | The oldest male in the group | The guard on duty that day
exercise: How is a naked mole rat colony like a beehive? | One queen has the babies and the others work | Everyone flies out to find flowers | They all make honey underground | Each animal lives alone in its own room
exercise: Why do naked mole rats pile up in a heap? | To keep warm, since they are poor at warming themselves | To hide from the zookeepers who come each morning to count them | To fight each other for the warm place at the very top | Because they are asleep almost all day and all night
exercise: About how long did naked mole rats survive with no oxygen in experiments? | About 18 minutes | About 18 seconds | About 18 hours | About 18 days
exercise: What happens to a naked mole rat from another colony? | It is known by its chirp accent and attacked | It is welcomed by everyone and soon becomes the new queen | It is fed first as an honoured guest | Nobody can tell it is a stranger, so it simply stays
exercise: What did experiments suggest about prairie dog alarm calls? | They can describe a predator's size and colour | They are all exactly the same squeak, whatever the danger is | Prairie dogs never call when danger comes | They only call when they are hungry and want food
exercise: Why do emperor penguins keep slowly moving in a huddle? | They take turns at the cold windy edge | They are dancing slowly round and round to find a mate | They are walking all together to the sea to eat | The ice is slowly melting away under their feet
exercise: Where does a male emperor penguin keep his egg in winter? | On his feet under a fold of skin | In a nest of stones | Buried in the snow | In the sea for the mother
exercise: What does a cleaner fish get from a big fish? | Food: the parasites it eats off the big fish | A free ride across the ocean on the big fish's back | Protection inside the big fish's stomach | Nothing at all, because it is forced to work
exercise: When do cleaner fish cheat less? | When other fish are watching | When they are very hungry indeed | At night, when the reef is dark | When the big fish is fast asleep
exercise: Why is the oxpecker only partly a helper? | It also pecks at wounds to drink blood | It also steals the buffalo's grass | It also bites the buffalo's ears off | It also scares other birds away
exercise: Where did the lemming cliff-jumping story come from? | A 1958 nature film where lemmings were pushed | Lemmings really jump off cliffs together every single spring | An old Viking law about lemmings | Scientists counting lemmings on beaches
exercise: How long can a naked mole rat live? | Over 30 years | About 3 years at most | About 3 months | About 300 years
test: In The Lion King a meerkat lives with a warthog and sings. What is a real meerkat's life like? | It lives in a big group led by a female, with guards and teachers | It lives alone in the jungle with a warthog friend and a lion cub, singing songs | It lives in a pair with a lion | It never meets other meerkats and digs on its own
test: «my mate reckons lemmings jump off cliffs coz there's too many of them, that's a fact innit» What is right? | No, it is a myth from a film where lemmings were pushed | Yes, they jump off cliffs every year to make room for the others | Yes, but only in Norway | No, lemmings jump into rivers instead of off cliffs
test: «7 meerkats on the rock, how many legs is that» | 28 | 14 | 21 | 35
test: «12 penguins in a huddle, 2 more join. how many legs, quick» | 28 | 24 | 56 | 14
test: «4 meerkats, 1 warthog. how many tails» | 5 | 4 | 8 | 1
test: What would happen if no meerkat stood guard? | An eagle could catch them while they dig with heads down | They would dig faster without stopping and be quite safe | The scorpions would protect them | Nothing, because eagles and jackals never hunt meerkats at all
test: «why the hell is that bird sat on a buffalo, piss off bird» What is it doing? | Eating ticks and flies, and sometimes drinking blood from wounds | Laying its eggs safely in the thick warm fur along the buffalo's back | Steering the buffalo like a rider on a horse | Waiting for the buffalo to die
test: «ugh that pink wrinkly rat thing is gross, prob dies young» What is true? | No, naked mole rats can live over 30 years and rarely get cancer | Yes, they live only a few weeks, just as most other small rodents do | Yes, they die very quickly if they ever see daylight | No, they often live for about 300 years, longer than tortoises
test: A big fish on a reef waits with its mouth open while a tiny fish swims inside. Why doesn't it swallow the tiny fish? | The tiny fish cleans it, so both gain | It has no teeth | It is not hungry at that moment | The tiny fish is poisonous
test: A naked mole rat baby is moved to a new colony. What happens to its chirps? | It learns the new colony's dialect | It keeps its old dialect and is attacked | It stops chirping for the rest of its life | It teaches its dialect to the queen
test: «a tall bloke in a blue shirt walked past the prairie dogs, then a short one in yellow. same squeak, right?» | No, experiments suggest the calls change with size and colour | Yes, prairie dogs have only one squeak, the same for any danger at all | No, they only squeak when they see dogs | Yes, they cannot see colours
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of a meerkat sentinel? | It maintains watch for predators while its companions forage, issuing distinct alarm calls | It consumes the food gathered by its companions while they are distracted | It remains permanently in that office throughout its whole life, never digging | It forages alone and avoids the group entirely
exam: Identify the principal reason for which emperor penguins assemble in dense huddles during the Antarctic winter. | The conservation of warmth, each bird taking its turn at the exposed margin | The concealment of the eggs from hungry seals and from the mothers | The facilitation of feeding upon fish beneath the ice | The selection of a leader for the colony
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the alleged mass suicide of lemmings. | The notion is a myth, disseminated by a film in which the animals were deliberately propelled | It is a well-documented annual occurrence, recorded each spring by naturalists in Norway and Sweden | It occurs solely when food is abundant | It has been observed exclusively in captivity, never in the wild
exam: Ascertain the benefit derived by each party in the association between the cleaner wrasse and its larger client fish. | The client is freed of parasites, whereas the cleaner obtains nourishment | Only the cleaner benefits, the client being harmed by its sharp little bites | Only the client benefits, the cleaner receiving nothing for its labours | Neither party derives any advantage whatsoever
